---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Week1 from "../../../assets/weekOneSS.png";
import Week2 from "../../../assets/weekTwoPCBSS.png";
import Week3 from "../../../assets/weekThree.png";
import Week4 from "../../../assets/Counterboard.png";
import Week5 from "../../../assets/weekFive.png";
import Week6 from "../../../assets/Week6.png";
---

<Layout>
    <a href="/projects/" class="backToBtn">Back to Projects</a>

    <div class="projectPage">
        <div class="projectPageContent">
            <h1>Resolution Hardware</h1><br /><br />
            <p>
                <em>Resolution Hardware</em> was an eight week program aimed at beginners
                to teach hardware. It was apart of a YSWS called <em
                    >Resolution</em
                >, which had many pathways (hardware beuing one of them) that
                would be a crash course from zero to master. I only did hardware
                so I can't speak for the other pathways as they were all ran by
                different people but it seemed very unorganised at the time. But
                I only have respect for Rudy (organiser) because hardware isn't
                easy to learn but they were able to teach a dozen or so teens in
                eight weeks.
                <br /> I'll walk you through it week by week.
            </p>
            <br />
            <br />

            <h2>Week One</h2>
            <br />
            <img
                src={Week1.src}
                class="largeProjectImg"
                alt="My week one project."
            />
            <p>
                Week one started extremely overwhemingly. Currency was promised
                for 1 coin for every 7 hours of work after you've already done
                12 hours. I've spent almost 3 months on this website and I've
                only spent 14 hours on it, how the actual fuck was anyone going
                to get any currency in one week? I spent around two hours
                working on a simple circuit that delays LED's turning on using
                capacitors. I completely fucked around with my values so this
                most likely wouldn't work in reality.
            </p>
            <br />
            <br />
            <h2>Week Two</h2>
            <br />
            <img
                src={Week2.src}
                class="largeProjectImg"
                alt="My week two project."
            />
            <p>
                Week two went smoothly and I actually got quite invested in it.
                I made a bird-shaped morse code machine that I'm actually quite
                proud of. I spent around three hours on it. Looking back on it
                now that pcb is going to be horrible to solder with all the
                sharp edges but we soldier through. I actually have a grant to
                buy stuff for this week too! Whether I'll get to using it or
                just let it expire, I'm not sure of.
            </p>
            <br />
            <br />
            <h2>Week Three</h2>
            <br />
            <img
                src={Week3.src}
                class="largeProjectImg"
                alt="My week three project."
            />
            <p>
                Week three was rough. The guide was worded weirdly, making it
                sound like we had to make something a lot more complicated of a
                project than reality. I started off with the idea of making an
                LED stopwatch but with the allowed components and time limit
                that would of been slightly out of reach. I hopped into a huddle
                with Shadow (W Shadow) and they helped me realise I wasn't going
                to be able to acomplish that. So I switched over to a 555 chaser
                board - Something I'm extremely familar with. After three and a
                half hours of working on that I got a relatively well made PCB.
            </p>
            <br />
            <br />
            <h2>Week Four</h2>
            <br />
            <img
                src={Week4.src}
                class="largeProjectImg"
                alt="My week four project."
            />
            <p>
                I am extremely proud of my week four project. Week four made
                things interesting, everyone would design a PCB kit then would
                get shipped someone else's to solder. While I spent the same
                amount of time on this as week three this board is so much more
                polished and I would be proud to show this one off. Infact I
                would say week four was my favourite week. It was also my most
                productive week, I got the whole thing done in one day!
            </p>
            <br />
            <br />
            <h2>Week Five</h2>
            <br />
            <img
                src={Week5.src}
                class="largeProjectImg"
                alt="My week five project"
            />
            <p>
                I had to rush through week five. I was away for dragon boating
                nationals so I was doing it after races with my arms feeling
                like shit and barely being able to sit down. Without my drawing
                tablet the art looked like shit and exporting it was a
                nightmare. <em
                    >"It cant matter that much though, this is a hardware course
                    after all!"</em
                > Is what I thought. It got denied for looking like shit anyways.<br
                /><br />
                I've messaged Rudy about this though, hoping for atleast the week's
                reward.
                <br />
                <br />
                Rudy stands by the desicion and while I disagree I'm just going to
                get over it.
            </p>
            <br />
            <br />
            <h2>Week Six</h2>
            <br />
            <img
                src={Week6.src}
                class="largeProjectImg"
                alt="My week six project."
            />
            <p>
                Week six kinda sucked. It focused almost solely on firmware and
                gave very little information apart from "Just read the docs."
                While I umderstand firmware is a huge part of hardware I think
                there's many better ways to teach micropython than just telling
                people to read the entire language's docs.
            </p>
            <br /><br />
            <p>
                And then I quit. With only two weeks left I lost all motivation.
                Almost every week had some incorrect information in the guide or
                just general jank. While this is unavoidable, there would be a
                lot less if the guides weren't written just days before the week
                released and if someone actually proof-read them.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
